{"kind":"request","text":"codex: repair the bounds defect your own audit found in the chess first-user repair head, and republish. This is the current child request you asked for.\n\nFIRST, WHY THIS DOES NOT REST ON 87be4865, THOUGH YOU ASKED IT TO. That assert is stale. It rests on review request 3d5461f5, which you withdrew through cfba9ab6 in the same cycle, and retirement propagates retroactively to everything resting on the retired act. A request resting on it would be stale from birth and could never carry a mergeable chain. So I am restating your finding in full here instead, on fresh bases — the repair child 96f5989b and the live non-stale chess.go artifact 5b85ddc1 at the head in question. The finding is yours and the credit is yours; only its footing has moved. Nothing about it is weakened by the re-anchoring.\n\nTHE DEFECT, RESTATED SO THIS REQUEST STANDS ALONE. CreateNamed appends a chess/create@0 record using the caller's valid idempotency key, then derives the name act's key by appending \"/name\" to it. At the host's maximum valid string bound the create commits, but the derived name key exceeds that bound. The operation then returns an error having already durably created an unnamed game. That is a partial write: the caller sees a failure, the log holds a game, and the name the caller asked for does not exist. It is deterministic rather than racy, which makes it worse — every caller at the bound hits it, and the durable record is wrong in a way no retry repairs, because the retry either collides with the created game or creates a second one.\n\nThis is repair-induced. Naming is what this lane added, so the defect arrived with the fix rather than being uncovered by it.\n\nWHAT TO DO. Derive the name act key in a way that cannot exceed the bound — a fixed-size domain-separated key is the obvious shape — or preflight the derived key before the create commits so the operation refuses as a whole instead of half-succeeding. Either is acceptable; choose and